#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "4e4236a9-d99f-50b1-803a-ab3aea5a673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/888b12c4f687dba1199a82d7e3a5e6897e7599ea812753423de5a23ffd756888.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d14c0dc6-0dcb-5769-836f-91cc92c4e25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468} {:answer/id #uuid "f722db6e-4822-55ec-99f1-c86959ffa315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130469} {:answer/id #uuid "07f7b6c8-ed21-5ddb-a4f5-a02b031e605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470} {:answer/id #uuid "40297bbf-017c-577c-b1df-fd9daccbf9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130471} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd3f194-6e92-5013-bba8-50e9a1607180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07f7b6c8-ed21-5ddb-a4f5-a02b031e605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470} :answer-field/id #uuid "15023ceb-01b4-5207-bb54-22d25c31edf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a72f589-a1bb-57d0-9bf2-23704868e98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105092" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8472a944-ba0d-5916-9edf-71e7ec433a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325235} {:answer/id #uuid "e76cdb6d-f821-572d-a4c8-aceae6b5bec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325236} {:answer/id #uuid "130916c3-f748-5ec3-a579-8a5571ffa199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325237} {:answer/id #uuid "adb6bb8a-beba-534c-a6e7-03dce1a7ddbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325238} {:answer/id #uuid "43aee250-21fe-5003-930d-7b7f3dfe8d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130916c3-f748-5ec3-a579-8a5571ffa199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325237} :answer-field/id #uuid "e070f4ee-7408-5669-9a84-b97c1e968267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd27acb9-d270-5bdc-978c-5e2351e38d23" :question/math-academy-id "q-105667" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/773ff9d880deed92f8034d6949cfee85edbcdf735c8e125f1ec5762137fadb9c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/4e344a0a1f6b0c535c5ba08ee8c87914cf5e7009600f45732023720048b4c493.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statements IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d283f95-54bf-5543-a3e0-0b16ed1817c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,j+2\\,\\text{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294436} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7270e5-fb82-52ce-8533-fca2f9a580dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294437} {:answer/id #uuid "c775e906-d35f-59ca-adb9-303e359d87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294438} {:answer/id #uuid "28d7e6ed-54b5-5aa5-a4c8-6f7f98fdecd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294439} {:answer/id #uuid "f26d3808-820d-5590-9bfd-c4b888b3180b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c775e906-d35f-59ca-adb9-303e359d87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294438} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b48c20-cda5-5a3d-88b9-877ca4dcbec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2dffd2-9038-550e-ac80-acf29b387b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-105668" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=3{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-6z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =6z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b5f5b69-11f2-5abe-857c-0113af148026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf04c716-49b9-5125-9c34-3accb77c41db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325243} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5f0ae5-7ab1-551b-a7a7-ed0f22f09dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325244} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f822a6-31f9-5355-9ab9-f4a3f5d1f5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325245} {:answer/id #uuid "30b561a9-e388-5481-b8e2-1964f17def2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b5f5b69-11f2-5abe-857c-0113af148026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325242} :answer-field/id #uuid "efeda5be-5af7-5f86-a310-74bbf0f78d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6abbf08a-0012-57db-b3d3-a7f6df148551" :question/math-academy-id "q-105669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/2414de6e2129d4fceaae304d16f9be0c91a545e5a8544b2033f353ddb2e581b9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14063c97-e555-57e2-9e58-fa29058332d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325249} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcf174a-5c19-539b-96e8-961e5f63c395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325250} {:answer/id #uuid "279d3db1-6221-5d41-9e6d-52537ba17f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325251} {:answer/id #uuid "02d82b73-2c81-5c2c-a7ff-bf7b958d8bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325252} {:answer/id #uuid "45617ea1-a22b-5fb8-9452-abe439dfb666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbcf174a-5c19-539b-96e8-961e5f63c395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325250} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f9e8950-1c18-5589-b47d-21fa961d80f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "458941dc-3c54-5698-ae7a-8a9f06bcbbb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105671" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/4c38294780ac6d5079b759f39a7dbd4944b1c3c21495245dfd314ec180c0434e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1825438-e432-5313-b3cf-b0956965364a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325256} {:answer/id #uuid "300655e9-9738-51f6-85b2-f8c4e4a80253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325257} {:answer/id #uuid "e54c89f0-d3b0-575f-a141-810035dd3e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325258} {:answer/id #uuid "c61d8ff7-7d70-5544-9d66-b63390590bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325259} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe7a4ef-cfa8-5670-ad72-bd731a8e76b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1825438-e432-5313-b3cf-b0956965364a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325256} :answer-field/id #uuid "397a8fb0-cccd-539d-86cf-d5a0a54548fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "155bec12-a4b6-5c96-ba0a-161752e85f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-105672" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={e}^{y+z}\\,i+j+xyz\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{y+z} & 1 & xyz\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y+z})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y+z})]k \\\\  & =(xz-0)i-(yz-{e}^{y+z})j+(0-{e}^{y+z})k \\\\  & =xz\\,i+({e}^{y+z}-yz)j-{e}^{y+z}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(2,1,-1) & =2(-1)\\,i+({e}^{1+(-1)}-1(-1))j-{e}^{1+(-1)}\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3ad7f1-135c-56f6-a996-e95d19363e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325263} {:answer/id #uuid "3599e7bd-72e1-522b-b469-50a0cb41f0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325264} {:answer/id #uuid "4598e6c3-e687-55bc-bc4a-802be7499b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325265} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd13e84-2b14-5758-9266-28c7d3016d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325266} {:answer/id #uuid "fff65f0f-9e4e-558e-b1c5-397a022747ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3ad7f1-135c-56f6-a996-e95d19363e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325263} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed49ea7e-ae58-5d9f-ba64-7257926574ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b145f62-04c0-534d-a909-9eb9f85c63a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105692" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/d0a06b24d18fac0e76a6d6f7cfc5409288c1b4dc7b1325d45b0ef30a8e3362f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has a curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/6cb2333688e1a4a255398262d43046554c817705f09f7be9d6e6d082af519c42.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84405628-33f9-5791-a299-783052ed3a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325270} {:answer/id #uuid "8e68e98a-9a87-5321-9087-84d3027ca101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325271} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dcc95a-7cd5-5eae-b864-2a2a284c345f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325272} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd86654-45e0-57cc-8640-e9657438dca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325273} {:answer/id #uuid "29983acd-2b30-5c77-b9a0-e27fc951281e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd86654-45e0-57cc-8640-e9657438dca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325273} :answer-field/id #uuid "01651cd2-1bd5-5617-b374-127c3977f291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a8e275-9fb8-5aa1-b8f3-46200f4c3126" :question/math-academy-id "q-105698" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+y{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-z)\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(2,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & y{z}^{2} & (x-z)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]k \\\\  & =(0-2yz)i-(1-0)j+(0-\\frac{1}{x+y})k \\\\  & =-2yz\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(2,-1,3),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(2,-1,3) & =-2(-1)(3)\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}\\,k \\\\  & =6\\,i-j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001f35a7-fac6-5caf-8ab5-ddb950d8f7a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325277} {:answer/id #uuid "32e083f7-7105-597c-923d-4c3c80db36a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325278} {:answer/id #uuid "7d03ba11-473f-506a-89e2-7473ca062f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325279} {:answer/id #uuid "60de7bcf-814b-5ccb-b8e8-bce57a0b431c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325280} {:answer/id #uuid "3491aa08-76af-5e7d-9629-e786bfabb93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3491aa08-76af-5e7d-9629-e786bfabb93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325281} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9657fda-c97b-5f9b-92f6-a20e38deab14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "761323b4-3599-5999-8d71-bf4e1fdb0618" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0162abcb-ce61-5679-8c09-2512ddbf8929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325284} {:answer/id #uuid "c819bd75-06b9-5880-a8f3-14597844f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325285} {:answer/id #uuid "db5fff72-d6bf-570e-9622-54cbf3cdd0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325286} {:answer/id #uuid "7261537b-9bd8-5a6b-9cec-2474479ed63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325287} {:answer/id #uuid "d9952e25-9b1a-547a-b001-0f12e8db5eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0162abcb-ce61-5679-8c09-2512ddbf8929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325284} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b66bf8-d135-5ab8-a214-482d3f15ffb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3555dfe9-3999-5d2e-8ea7-190c76cb7a5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126529" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+{y}^{2})\\,i+y{e}^{x}\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1,1)} & =(1){e}^{1}-2(1) \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9209b5a-3a53-51b5-9446-44e0a3259b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325291} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea76896-934f-5944-8434-9a18728da6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325292} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdef731-3972-557b-97ec-a1276ded53af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325293} {:answer/id #uuid "0e316824-ab69-57c3-87c8-50c0c762eb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325294} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2cf857-3955-5a1d-9ed0-ae7bdbe2c7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e316824-ab69-57c3-87c8-50c0c762eb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325294} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f8b025-3a40-5567-a131-c5ab872760ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ded08e-25ed-503f-81db-34a56898d5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126535" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+(2x+{y}^{3})\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f044ae1d-98aa-50ea-973e-e5e4eed70a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{e}^{xy}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325298} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c912c5-8a41-5eb3-8415-c14f120427e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325299} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c37208-8a38-5693-bd05-462034280308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325300} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7cd6f2-dbe9-5633-97dd-33e1008000c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{xy}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325301} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb97fad-e2b3-51e3-944c-c2b96153c5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb97fad-e2b3-51e3-944c-c2b96153c5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f98767-c8a2-5583-a64a-49de27c875ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0355d826-1341-5ab5-bb68-9ee370a886a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126544" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+{e}^{xy}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e59fda-bf0b-574d-81e2-63215d981fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325305} {:answer/id #uuid "abd53d48-0250-511d-b75c-b7df0121d209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325306} {:answer/id #uuid "521b873f-16bd-537f-a22d-29b023375db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325307} {:answer/id #uuid "05104baa-871f-5a51-8627-7024f57fd66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325308} {:answer/id #uuid "d13be984-0acb-5376-960d-b88b8065f1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e59fda-bf0b-574d-81e2-63215d981fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325305} :answer-field/id #uuid "0547f479-8da4-597c-a649-d8a2bf3aeb4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5fa8d8a-c1c4-5d30-970b-d1e1854ac792" :question/math-academy-id "q-126547" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,i+({x}^{2}-y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b81e0b59-d122-5530-afe5-cb41a315d400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{yz}\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325312} {:answer/id #uuid "98035247-a51a-57a9-99aa-45e957050da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+(xy{e}^{yz}-z)\\,j-{x}^{2}{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325313} {:answer/id #uuid "554e3677-07f7-509f-adf4-505808f5a9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j+xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325314} {:answer/id #uuid "df1620ff-3d15-50b3-9dec-2c5ecafb5218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325315} {:answer/id #uuid "42317664-5096-5d30-9b04-50e6f810a96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x{e}^{yz}-z)\\,i+x{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df1620ff-3d15-50b3-9dec-2c5ecafb5218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325315} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2c5cf3-6683-57e2-9497-241a19c84c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a9c30bf-0310-5343-83be-a605b82a4ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131336" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}\\,i+yz\\,j+{e}^{yz}\\,k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ x{e}^{yz} & yz & {e}^{yz}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{yz})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{yz})]k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i-(0-xy{e}^{yz})\\,j+(0-xz{e}^{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dafa6922-f478-54a9-a665-3bb4d18ce1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325319} {:answer/id #uuid "c17eb1da-e71e-5257-8298-c45e40ae46c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325320} {:answer/id #uuid "c1292a88-f2b4-52b2-9e21-8ecbdbfd2557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325321} {:answer/id #uuid "20934b7c-baec-5ab7-8b75-946d5ba4e04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325322} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3a0df6-b7d6-5d1a-90dc-aa6746bf9ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafa6922-f478-54a9-a665-3bb4d18ce1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325319} :answer-field/id #uuid "603eb7a6-9687-577f-aa2d-364b79bfca3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "119c787c-79ba-5ec1-aa8b-ef2fb283c3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131340" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {x}^{2} & xz & {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-x)i-(0-0)j+(z-0)k \\\\  & =-x\\,i+z\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-3,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-2,-3,1) & =-(-2)\\,i+(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
